name: {{ CLI_NAME }}-brainstorm
description: "Brainstorm a plan using {{ CLI_NAME }} outputs"
---
Brainstorm a plan using {{ CLI_NAME }} outputs.

1. Explore the project context:
   - Check recent commits, existing files, and docs.
   - Run `{{ CLI_NAME }} output search -q "<what the user wants to plan>"` to see if any
     outputs already relate to this area.
   - If the search returns "No matching outputs found", check whether
     `{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ OUTPUTS_FOLDER }}/` exists and contains any
     `instructions.json` files. This distinguishes "no outputs exist yet"
     (greenfield — the first outputs in the project) from "outputs exist
     but none match this request."

2. Ask the user clarifying questions — one at a time — to understand:
   - What they are trying to achieve
   - What constraints apply
   - What success looks like

3. Identify the key intents (topics, domains, capabilities) this plan touches.
   For each intent, run `{{ CLI_NAME }} output search -q "<intent>"` to find
   existing outputs that may be reusable.

4. From the search results, determine:
   - Which existing outputs can be reused for this plan (list by name)
   - What work doesn't fit any existing output

5. For work that doesn't fit an existing output, classify each piece as a
   one-off task or a output capture candidate.

   A task is a good output capture candidate if:
   - It generates files with a repeating structure (same file types, same wiring)
   - It will likely be done again with different variable values
   - The structure is stable enough that parameterizing it adds value

   A task is one-off if:
   - It's project configuration (tsconfig, path aliases, package.json)
   - It's a bridge or adapter specific to this project's setup
   - It's content (locale strings, copy text, data files)
   - It's a one-time wiring or edit

   IMPORTANT: Follow the build-first principle. Do NOT plan to create
   outputs before the concrete implementation exists and is verified.
   Instead, plan the concrete implementation (what files to write, what they
   contain). Mark repeatable parts as output capture candidates — to be
   captured AFTER the implementation is verified working, using:
   `{{ CLI_NAME }} output gen -n <short-name> -i "<intent>" -v "<vars>" -o '<files-json>'`
   Then author the template at
   `{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ OUTPUTS_FOLDER }}/<name>/<template>`. Prefer a `.ts`
   file with a default-export function `(vars: Record<string, string>) => string` —
   this is the recommended format for new outputs. The CLI calls that function
   with the output's declared variables (keyed by name, all strings) and
   writes the returned string to `outputPath`; keep it a pure function of `vars`
   (no imports, no filesystem access). Example:
   `export default ({ name }: Record<string, string>) => \`# ${name}\`;`.
   Nunjucks `.njk` templates (`{{ '{{var}}' }}` syntax) are supported but should
   only be used when a `.ts` template is impractical. Validate with
   `{{ CLI_NAME }} output validate -n <name>`.

   Suboutputs: Do NOT plan suboutput decomposition upfront. Plan
   concrete, standalone outputs. If two planned outputs clearly share
   a file structure, note it as a future suboutput extraction candidate
   — to be extracted after both outputs are built and verified working.
   See the main instruction file for suboutput mechanics.

6. Present the plan in sections, covering:
   - Overview of the approach
   - Existing outputs to reuse (with names)
   - Concrete implementation steps (what files to write or modify)
   - Output capture candidates (to capture after implementation is verified,
     with suggested name, intent keywords, and variables)
   - Non-output work (one-off tasks)
   - Suggested order of implementation

   Present only the final plan. Do not include your reasoning process, internal
   notes, or chain-of-thought in the output.

7. Get user approval on the plan before proceeding.

8. Once approved, save the plan to a spec file at
   `docs/{{ CLI_NAME }}/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md` and commit it.
   Then transition to implementation by following the `{{ CLI_NAME }}-feature`
   command workflow.